; netstack.prefs - bsdsocket.library TCP/IP stack configuration (STACK-WIDE)
;
; The stack reads ENV:netstack.prefs once at startup (the first OpenLibrary
; of bsdsocket.library). Keep the master copy in ENVARC: so it survives
; reboots. Every key is optional; sensible defaults apply when a key (or
; this whole file) is absent.
;
; NETWORK INTERFACES ARE NOT CONFIGURED HERE. Each interface has its own
; file in DEVS:NetInterfaces/ (the file name is the interface name) and is
; added at runtime with the AddNetInterface command - the boot line lives in
; S:Network-Startup:
;   AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/~(#?.info) QUIET
; See the sample in SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/genet. The old interface keys
; (DEVICE, UNIT, MODE, ADDRESS, NETMASK, GATEWAY, VLAN) are obsolete and
; ignored here.
;
; HOSTNAME = amiga                  ; DHCP option 12 / gethostname(); an
;                                   ; interface file's ID= overrides it there
; DOMAIN = example.com              ; resolver search domain: dot-less names
;                                   ; are retried as "name.DOMAIN" on a miss
;
; Explicit DNS servers (optional). They override whatever a DHCP lease
; supplies, on every interface:
; DNS1 = 192.168.1.1
; DNS2 = 9.9.9.9
;
; mDNS / DNS-SD: the stack answers for HOSTNAME.local (Bonjour/Avahi), so the
; Amiga is reachable by name with no DNS server. MDNS_SERVICE advertises a
; service too (repeatable, up to 4): type, port, and an optional instance name
; that defaults to the host name. Services can also be registered while the
; stack runs - see the `mdns` command.
; MDNS = yes
; MDNS_SERVICE = _ftp._tcp 21
; MDNS_SERVICE = _telnet._tcp 23 Amiga 1200
;
; Networks database (getnetbyname/getnetbyaddr): built-in entries are
; "default 0" and "loopback 127"; NETWORK adds more (repeatable, up to 8,
; /etc/networks notation - the number is a classful NETWORK number like
; "192.168.0", not an IP address). Config entries shadow the built-ins.
; NETWORK = homelan 192.168.0
; NETWORK = officelan 10
